# Structured document conversion using AST: Carve and peer libraries

2026-10-24. This run used three public Carve documentation files from source commit `a91beafced99d738e5eee950b9ac1615983add5b`: [Terms](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/blob/a91beafced99d738e5eee950b9ac1615983add5b/docs/terms.md), [Parsing & AST](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/blob/a91beafced99d738e5eee950b9ac1615983add5b/docs/case-study/parsing-ast.md), and [Spec history](https://github.com/markup-carve/carve/blob/a91beafced99d738e5eee950b9ac1615983add5b/docs/spec-history.md). Each was rendered from native Carve, Djot, or Markdown source derived once from that file. The [fixture record](../corpus/realistic/peer/provenance.json) gives source and derived hashes. Markdown copies omit YAML front matter; Carve copies use the pinned `markdownToCarve` converter, and Djot copies use Pandoc 3.11 from GFM.

![Peer speed relative to Carve on three authored documents](../charts/realistic-peer-cpu.svg)

The chart shows each peer’s speed relative to Carve within 16 rotating rounds. Carve is 1×; a point above 1× means the peer used less process CPU time. Whiskers show the range of the round ratios. All three pinned Carve default APIs took the AST route on all three files. The peer libraries used their native HTML conversion APIs; this does not imply that each peer builds the same public AST.

This run used a load ceiling of 200, versus 20 in the preceding published snapshot. One-minute load at document checkpoints was 27.07–84.10. Read the peer ratios within this run; the engine pins and host load differ from the preceding chart.

## Results

The inputs are 3.1 to 19.3 KiB, depending on syntax. The table gives pooled median process CPU milliseconds per conversion, then the median of within-round peer/Carve speed ratios and its round range. Compare rows within a runtime and document. JavaScript process CPU includes Node threads. PHP used tracing JIT. Rust used a release binary. Startup time was excluded.

